% ---------------------------------------------------------------- coordinate_descent
%
%   CoordinateDescent on the DifferentiableFunction callback interface.
%   Each epoch visits coordinates in index order. Every partial update requests a full gradient and an Armijo-backtracked value sequence; this is not exact one-dimensional minimisation. An exhausted search restores that coordinate. Stops on the full end-of-epoch gradient norm.
%   The caller supplies finite, dimensionally consistent callbacks. Inputs are borrowed;
%   coordinates mutate in place and can retain a partial state if a callback throws.
%   Calls use local optimizer state and propagate callback exceptions; sharing a mutable
%   objective across threads requires external synchronisation. Normal return alone
%   is not a uniform convergence certificate.
%
%   Wraps the C++ class `datamunge::optim::CoordinateDescent`.
%
%   *Example*
%
%   ==
%   const dm::optim::CoordinateDescent optimizer;
%   std::print("{}\n",optimizer.options().max_iterations); // 1000
%   ==
%
%   *References*
%
%     - S. J. Wright (2015), Coordinate descent algorithms, https://doi.org/10.1007/s10107-015-0892-3. This implementation uses the stated full-gradient coordinate/block steps.

%!  coordinate_descent_new(-Object:coordinate_descent) is det.
%!  coordinate_descent_new(+Options:coordinate_descent_options, -Object:coordinate_descent) is det.
%
%   Store a copy of the controls without running the objective.
%
%   Calls the C++ constructor `datamunge::optim::CoordinateDescent`.
%
%   *Example*
%
%   ==
%   const dm::optim::CoordinateDescent optimizer;
%   std::print("{}\n",optimizer.options().max_iterations); // 1000
%   ==
%
%   Arguments left out take the C++ defaults: Options = C++ default.
%
%   @arg Options Method-specific controls; defaults are listed by CoordinateDescentOptions.
%   @see `optimize()`
coordinate_descent_new(Object) :-
    '$rosetta_construct'(1660, [], [], [], Object).
coordinate_descent_new(Options, Object) :-
    '$rosetta_construct'(1660, [Options], [], [], Object).

%!  coordinate_descent_optimize(+CoordinateDescent, +Function:differentiable_function, +Coordinates0:list(float), -Coordinates:list(float), -Result:float) is det.
%
%   Run CoordinateDescent from the supplied point using the configured finite budget.
%   Each epoch visits coordinates in index order. Every partial update requests a full gradient and an Armijo-backtracked value sequence; this is not exact one-dimensional minimisation. An exhausted search restores that coordinate. Stops on the full end-of-epoch gradient norm.
%
%   Calls the C++ method `datamunge::optim::CoordinateDescent::optimize`.
%
%   *Example*
%
%   ==
%   dm::optim::QuadraticObjective f({2,8},{1,-2});
%   dm::optim::CoordinateDescentOptions controls;controls.max_iterations=5;
%   std::vector<double> x{0,0};
%   const auto value=dm::optim::CoordinateDescent(controls).optimize(f,x);
%   std::print("{}\n",std::isfinite(value)); // true
%   ==
%
%   @arg Function Borrowed DifferentiableFunction with matching finite output shapes.
%   @arg Coordinates0 Owned-by-caller finite start; overwritten with the native returned point.; C++ writes it back: Coordinates is the value after the call
%   @arg Result Objective at the native returned coordinates; not a stop-status code.
%   @throws error(native_error(invalid_argument, Message), _) (C++ `std::invalid_argument`): CoordinateDescent: step_size must be positive; CoordinateDescent: tolerance must be non-negative; CoordinateDescent: armijo_c1 must be in (0, 1); CoordinateDescent: backtracking_factor must be in (0, 1).
%   @throws error(native_error(native, Message), _) (C++ `std::exception`): Exceptions from supplied callbacks propagate. Raw solvers do not uniformly reject nonfinite inputs.
%   @see `trace_optimize()`, coordinate_descent_options_new/1
coordinate_descent_optimize(CoordinateDescent, Function, Coordinates0, Coordinates, Result) :-
    '$rosetta_call'(1658, [CoordinateDescent, Function, '$rosetta_slot'(0, Coordinates0)], Result, Writebacks),
    '$rosetta_out'(Writebacks, 0, Coordinates0, Coordinates).

