% ---------------------------------------------------------------- levenberg_marquardt
%
%   LevenbergMarquardt on the ResidualFunction callback interface.
%   Forms J transpose J plus scalar damping*I and solves by unpivoted elimination. Accepts only strict reductions of half residual SSE, multiplying damping by .3 on acceptance and 10 on rejection. Tests proposed step norm even after rejection. A small rejected step is not a gradient certificate; no parameter constraints. O(np^2+p^3) work.
%   The caller supplies finite, dimensionally consistent callbacks. Inputs are borrowed;
%   coordinates mutate in place and can retain a partial state if a callback throws.
%   Calls use local optimizer state and propagate callback exceptions; sharing a mutable
%   objective across threads requires external synchronisation. Normal return alone
%   is not a uniform convergence certificate.
%
%   Wraps the C++ class `datamunge::optim::LevenbergMarquardt`.
%
%   *Example*
%
%   ==
%   const dm::optim::LevenbergMarquardt optimizer;
%   std::print("{}\n",optimizer.options().max_iterations); // 200
%   ==
%
%   *References*
%
%     - K. Levenberg (1944), A Method for the Solution of Certain Non-Linear Problems in Least Squares, https://doi.org/10.1090/qam/10666; D. W. Marquardt (1963), An Algorithm for Least-Squares Estimation of Nonlinear Parameters, https://doi.org/10.1137/0111030

%!  levenberg_marquardt_new(-Object:levenberg_marquardt) is det.
%!  levenberg_marquardt_new(+Options:levenberg_marquardt_options, -Object:levenberg_marquardt) is det.
%
%   Store a copy of the controls without running the objective.
%
%   Calls the C++ constructor `datamunge::optim::LevenbergMarquardt`.
%
%   *Example*
%
%   ==
%   const dm::optim::LevenbergMarquardt optimizer;
%   std::print("{}\n",optimizer.options().max_iterations); // 200
%   ==
%
%   Arguments left out take the C++ defaults: Options = C++ default.
%
%   @arg Options Method-specific controls; defaults are listed by LevenbergMarquardtOptions.
%   @see `optimize()`
levenberg_marquardt_new(Object) :-
    '$rosetta_construct'(1941, [], [], [], Object).
levenberg_marquardt_new(Options, Object) :-
    '$rosetta_construct'(1941, [Options], [], [], Object).

%!  levenberg_marquardt_optimize(+LevenbergMarquardt, +Function:residual_function, +Coordinates0:list(float), -Coordinates:list(float), -Result:float) is det.
%
%   Run LevenbergMarquardt from the supplied point using the configured finite budget.
%   Forms J transpose J plus scalar damping*I and solves by unpivoted elimination. Accepts only strict reductions of half residual SSE, multiplying damping by .3 on acceptance and 10 on rejection. Tests proposed step norm even after rejection. A small rejected step is not a gradient certificate; no parameter constraints. O(np^2+p^3) work.
%
%   Calls the C++ method `datamunge::optim::LevenbergMarquardt::optimize`.
%
%   *Example*
%
%   ==
%   const auto data=dm::optim::optimization_curve_data();dm::optim::CurveResidual f(data);
%   dm::optim::LevenbergMarquardtOptions controls;controls.max_iterations=5;
%   std::vector<double> x{2,.5};
%   const auto value=dm::optim::LevenbergMarquardt(controls).optimize(f,x);
%   std::print("{}\n",std::isfinite(value)); // true
%   ==
%
%   @arg Function Borrowed ResidualFunction with matching finite output shapes.
%   @arg Coordinates0 Owned-by-caller finite start; overwritten with the native returned point.; C++ writes it back: Coordinates is the value after the call
%   @arg Result Objective at the native returned coordinates (half residual SSE); not a stop-status code.
%   @throws error(native_error(invalid_argument, Message), _) (C++ `std::invalid_argument`): LevenbergMarquardt: initial_damping must be positive.
%   @throws error(native_error(native, Message), _) (C++ `std::exception`): Exceptions from supplied callbacks propagate. Raw solvers do not uniformly reject nonfinite inputs.
%   @see `trace_optimize()`, levenberg_marquardt_options_new/1
levenberg_marquardt_optimize(LevenbergMarquardt, Function, Coordinates0, Coordinates, Result) :-
    '$rosetta_call'(1939, [LevenbergMarquardt, Function, '$rosetta_slot'(0, Coordinates0)], Result, Writebacks),
    '$rosetta_out'(Writebacks, 0, Coordinates0, Coordinates).

