% ---------------------------------------------------------------- proximal_gradient
%
%   ProximalGradient on the ProximalFunction callback interface.
%   ISTA update prox(x-step_size*gradient(x),step_size), with gradient of the smooth part only. Stops on coordinate displacement norm. There is no Lipschitz estimation or backtracking; caller chooses a stable rate. The returned objective includes both smooth and nonsmooth parts.
%   The caller supplies finite, dimensionally consistent callbacks. Inputs are borrowed;
%   coordinates mutate in place and can retain a partial state if a callback throws.
%   Calls use local optimizer state and propagate callback exceptions; sharing a mutable
%   objective across threads requires external synchronisation. Normal return alone
%   is not a uniform convergence certificate.
%
%   Wraps the C++ class `datamunge::optim::ProximalGradient`.
%
%   *Example*
%
%   ==
%   const dm::optim::ProximalGradient optimizer;
%   std::print("{}\n",optimizer.options().max_iterations); // 10000
%   ==
%
%   *References*
%
%     - A. Beck and M. Teboulle (2009), A Fast Iterative Shrinkage-Thresholding Algorithm for Linear Inverse Problems, https://doi.org/10.1137/080716542

%!  proximal_gradient_new(-Object:proximal_gradient) is det.
%!  proximal_gradient_new(+Options:proximal_gradient_options, -Object:proximal_gradient) is det.
%
%   Store a copy of the controls without running the objective.
%
%   Calls the C++ constructor `datamunge::optim::ProximalGradient`.
%
%   *Example*
%
%   ==
%   const dm::optim::ProximalGradient optimizer;
%   std::print("{}\n",optimizer.options().max_iterations); // 10000
%   ==
%
%   Arguments left out take the C++ defaults: Options = C++ default.
%
%   @arg Options Method-specific controls; defaults are listed by ProximalGradientOptions.
%   @see `optimize()`
proximal_gradient_new(Object) :-
    '$rosetta_construct'(2051, [], [], [], Object).
proximal_gradient_new(Options, Object) :-
    '$rosetta_construct'(2051, [Options], [], [], Object).

%!  proximal_gradient_optimize(+ProximalGradient, +Function:proximal_function, +Coordinates0:list(float), -Coordinates:list(float), -Result:float) is det.
%
%   Run ProximalGradient from the supplied point using the configured finite budget.
%   ISTA update prox(x-step_size*gradient(x),step_size), with gradient of the smooth part only. Stops on coordinate displacement norm. There is no Lipschitz estimation or backtracking; caller chooses a stable rate. The returned objective includes both smooth and nonsmooth parts.
%
%   Calls the C++ method `datamunge::optim::ProximalGradient::optimize`.
%
%   *Example*
%
%   ==
%   dm::optim::L1LeastSquaresObjective f({{1}},{3},1);
%   dm::optim::ProximalGradientOptions controls;controls.max_iterations=5;
%   std::vector<double> x{0};
%   const auto value=dm::optim::ProximalGradient(controls).optimize(f,x);
%   std::print("{}\n",std::isfinite(value)); // true
%   ==
%
%   @arg Function Borrowed ProximalFunction with matching finite output shapes.
%   @arg Coordinates0 Owned-by-caller finite start; overwritten with the native returned point.; C++ writes it back: Coordinates is the value after the call
%   @arg Result Objective at the native returned coordinates; not a stop-status code.
%   @throws error(native_error(invalid_argument, Message), _) (C++ `std::invalid_argument`): ProximalGradient: step_size must be positive; ProximalGradient: proximal returned wrong dimension.
%   @throws error(native_error(native, Message), _) (C++ `std::exception`): Exceptions from supplied callbacks propagate. Raw solvers do not uniformly reject nonfinite inputs.
%   @see `trace_optimize()`, proximal_gradient_options_new/1
proximal_gradient_optimize(ProximalGradient, Function, Coordinates0, Coordinates, Result) :-
    '$rosetta_call'(2049, [ProximalGradient, Function, '$rosetta_slot'(0, Coordinates0)], Result, Writebacks),
    '$rosetta_out'(Writebacks, 0, Coordinates0, Coordinates).

